{"author":{"id":"f4f7e123ea38957901744390924e1710825e6d55b5113472173231f66fb2d926"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1781371122,"nonce":"2NJJvw3quhTjKA488oOEwpmLTBw=","title":"Compatibility corpus: 101 BASIC Computer Games (1978 microcomputer edition)","message":"Pull the ~100 original Microsoft-dialect listings from the 1978 Creative Computing 'BASIC Computer Games' (David Ahl) -- the canonical programs of the GW-BASIC era. Best modern source: github.com/coding-horror/basic-computer-games, which preserves each game's original BASIC alongside modern ports.\n\nGoal: run the corpus through BOTH the interpreter (gwbasic) and the AOT compiler (gwbasic-compile) and produce a pass/fail compatibility matrix; triage failures (genuine compat gaps vs. dialect quirks needing input stubs); fix the gaps. Turns 'bug-compatible with GW-BASIC' from a claim into evidence, hardens interp+compiler against real programs, and yields demo/Show HN material ('runs the original 101 BASIC games').\n\nNeighbor context: git.sdf.org/rfivet/BCG101 is the DEC *ancestor* (1975 3rd-print, TSS8 EduSystem / RSTS-E BASIC-PLUS dialect -- NOT directly runnable). The 1978 micro edition is the Microsoft-dialect sibling and is the right corpus here.\n\nSteps: (1) corpus harness that vendors the listings + canonical expected output where available; (2) batch-run through interp + compiler, results matrix; (3) triage genuine compat gaps; (4) fix; (5) optionally curate a subset into examples/ for the demo gallery. Many games need INPUT, so the harness needs scripted-input fixtures (reuse the dos_smoke OPEN/PRINT# capture pattern or feed stdin).","files":null}]}